# v0.2 result values (D3): `[ body ] at` runs the body on an isolated stack and
# yields ok(value) or err(code). The payload is reachable only through `??`
# with [ok]/[err] arms, so the failure path can never be skipped by accident.
5 :x 2 :y
[ x y / ] at .                                   # ok(2.5)
0 :y
[ x y / ] at .                                   # err(DIV_ZERO)
2 :y
[ x y / ] at [ [ok] [ 2 * . ] [err] [ . ] ] ??   # 5   (the ok arm gets the unwrapped 2.5)
0 :y
[ x y / ] at [ [ok] [ . ] [err] [ . ] ] ??       # DIV_ZERO (the err arm gets the code)
[ x y / ] at [ [res] [ , $a result$ . ] ] ??     # [res] is the type pattern (no unwrap)
[ x y / ] at ! .                                 # 1   a result is truthy iff ok
[ x y / ] at [ $good$ ] [ $bad$ ] ? @ .          # bad (branch on ok-ness without unwrapping)
[ `s rq 1 ] at [ [err] [ . ] [ok] [ , ] ] ??     # CAP_DENIED — a security fault is a value too
[ [ x y / ] at ] at .                            # ok(err(DIV_ZERO)) — results nest
7 [ 1 ] at , .                                   # 7   the outer stack is untouched
